For transitionStatus: only when target === "closed" (from open). Write snapshot in same transaction.

Digest computation: 
```ts
function snapshotDigest(pollId, groupVersion, total, options, closedAt) {
  return createHash("sha256").update(JSON.stringify({ pollId, groupVersion, total, options, closedAt }), "utf8").digest("hex");
}
```

buildSnapshot:
```ts
function buildSnapshot(pollId: string, closedAt: string): PollSnapshot {
  const row = pollRow(pollId)!;
  const options = JSON.parse(row.options_json) as { id: string }[];
  const counts = new Map(...votes group by...);
  const optionCounts = options.map(o => ({ id: o.id, count: counts.get(o.id) ?? 0 }));
  const total = optionCounts.reduce((s, o) => s + o.count, 0);
  const snapshot = { pollId, groupVersion: row.group_version, total, options: optionCounts, closedAt };
  return { ...snapshot, digest: digestOf(snapshot) };
}
```
Careful: digest over JSON.stringify of object with exactly the five fields in order. If I build `{pollId, groupVersion, total, options, closedAt}` then spread + digest, the JSON key order is insertion order — good.

Backfill in openCatalog after other migrations:
```ts
const missingSnapshots = db.prepare("SELECT * FROM polls WHERE status IN ('closed','archived') AND snapshot_json IS NULL").all() as PollRow[];
if (missingSnapshots.length > 0) {
  db.exec("BEGIN");
  try {
    for (const row of missingSnapshots) {
      const auditAt = db.prepare("SELECT at FROM audit_events WHERE poll_id = ? AND action = 'poll_status_change' AND result = 'success' AND json_extract(details_json, '$.to') = 'closed' ORDER BY at ASC, id ASC LIMIT 1").get(row.id) as { at: string } | undefined;
      const lastVote = db.prepare("SELECT accepted_at FROM votes WHERE poll_id = ? ORDER BY accepted_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 1").get(row.id) as { accepted_at: string } | undefined;
      const closedAt = new Date(Date.parse(auditAt?.at ?? lastVote?.accepted_at ?? row.closes_at)).toISOString();
      const snapshot = buildSnapshot(row.id, closedAt);
      db.prepare("UPDATE polls SET snapshot_json = ? WHERE id = ?").run(JSON.stringify(snapshot), row.id);
    }
    db.exec("COMMIT");
  } catch (e) { db.exec("ROLLBACK"); db.close(); throw e; }
}
```
Note: buildSnapshot uses pollRow — fine. But careful: buildSnapshot defined after? Order of function declarations — function declarations hoist, but these are const arrow/function inside openCatalog. `function` declarations hoist within the function scope, so fine.

Does SQLite json_extract work in node:sqlite? Yes, SQLite has JSON1 built in by default. But to be safe, I could instead filter in JS: select all successful poll_status_change events for the poll and check details.to === "closed". Simpler and avoids JSON1 dependency concerns. There won't be many. I'll do JS filtering.

closedAt normalization: `new Date(Date.parse(x)).toISOString()` — gives `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss.sssZ`. 

For new closes, `new Date(now).toISOString()` already matches format.

**results()**:
```ts
results(pollId, now) {
  closeExpired(now);
  const row = pollRow(pollId);
  if (!row || row.status === "draft") return undefined;
  ...counts...
  const result: PollResults = { pollId, total, options };
  if (row.status === "closed" || row.status === "archived") {
    const snapshot = row.snapshot_json ? JSON.parse(row.snapshot_json) as PollSnapshot : undefined;
    if (snapshot) result.snapshot = snapshot;
  }
  return result;
}
```
Note closeExpired may close the poll; pollRow is read after closeExpired so row reflects new state and snapshot_json was written in that transaction. Good.

